WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.716
It's the last day before the holidays, and the post office queue runs out of the door.

2
00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:13.316
Our quote service already gives each courier its own limit, and it asks again when a call fails.

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:16.075
But Northline has a different habit.

4
00:00:16.075 --> 00:00:19.394
Most of the time it answers in 40 milliseconds.

5
00:00:19.394 --> 00:00:21.441
One call in twenty takes 600.

6
00:00:21.441 --> 00:00:22.430
Nothing fails.

7
00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.748
Northline is just lost in thought now and then.

8
00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:27.646
So a retry doesn't help.

9
00:00:27.646 --> 00:00:31.913
A retry fires when a call fails, and this call hasn't failed.

10
00:00:31.913 --> 00:00:32.963
It's only slow.

11
00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:37.677
Let's draw twenty calls, and add one more word.

12
00:00:37.677 --> 00:00:41.325
A percentile says how slow the slow ones are.

13
00:00:41.325 --> 00:00:46.109
p50 is the time half the calls beat: here, 40 milliseconds.

14
00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:50.000
p99 is the time 99 calls in 100 beat: here, 600.

15
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:56.319
The slowest few calls are the tail, and tail latency is what the unluckiest customers see.

16
00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:58.618
One customer in twenty isn't rare.

17
00:00:58.618 --> 00:01:00.579
It's somebody in every queue.

18
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.064
So here's a question that feels a bit like cheating.

19
00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:07.076
If Northline is slow, why not just ask it again?

20
00:01:07.076 --> 00:01:09.524
And if we do, don't we double the work?

21
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.487
Let's try the bold version first.

22
00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:15.946
Ask twice, every time.

23
00:01:15.946 --> 00:01:22.176
Send the second question after 30 milliseconds, before Northline would normally have answered.

24
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.408
The lesson's demo counted.

25
00:01:24.408 --> 00:01:27.419
For 200 prices, 400 calls to Northline.

26
00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:32.361
Twice the load, every single time, to rescue one call in twenty.

27
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:37.433
That's like posting every parcel twice because the postman once took a long lunch.

28
00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:41.647
The second question should go only to the calls that are actually late.

29
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:43.613
Back to the cook.

30
00:01:43.613 --> 00:01:47.859
She texts a shop, and shops nearly always reply within a few moments.

31
00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:52.782
If this one hasn't replied by then, she texts the other branch of the same shop.

32
00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:57.889
Whichever branch answers first, she takes, and she tells the other one: never mind.

33
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:58.970
Let's map it.

34
00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.179
The first text is the first call.

35
00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:06.133
The few moments are the hedge time, taken from Northline's measured speed.

36
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:08.141
The demo uses 80 milliseconds.

37
00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:11.756
The second text is the hedge, one more identical call.

38
00:02:11.756 --> 00:02:14.032
Taking the first answer is select.

39
00:02:14.032 --> 00:02:16.643
And never mind is cancelling the loser.

40
00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:18.776
Now the slow call.

41
00:02:18.776 --> 00:02:22.472
The first question goes out at zero and would take 600.

42
00:02:22.472 --> 00:02:25.160
What's the longest a customer waits now?

43
00:02:28.133 --> 00:02:30.549
At 80 milliseconds, still nothing.

44
00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:32.113
So the hedge goes out.

45
00:02:32.113 --> 00:02:36.661
It's a normal call, so it answers 40 milliseconds later, at 120.

46
00:02:36.661 --> 00:02:40.427
The hedge wins, and the slow first call is cancelled.

47
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:43.901
80 of waiting, plus 40 for the hedge.

48
00:02:43.901 --> 00:02:46.727
120 milliseconds instead of 600.

49
00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:55.360
The demo asked Northline for 200 prices, without a hedge and with a hedge at 80.

50
00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.905
p50 stays at 40.

51
00:02:56.905 --> 00:02:59.416
p99 falls from 600 to 120.

52
00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:02.700
And the calls: 210 instead of 200.

53
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:08.356
Ten extra calls, 5 percent more, because only the slow 5 percent ever got a hedge.

54
00:03:08.356 --> 00:03:10.604
That's the answer to our question.

55
00:03:10.604 --> 00:03:12.257
We don't double the work.

56
00:03:12.257 --> 00:03:15.035
We spend a little, exactly where it hurts.

57
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:23.269
This move is called a hedge, like hedging a bet: a small second bet, so that one bad outcome can't hurt you much.

58
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.045
In Kotlin: start the first call.

59
00:03:26.045 --> 00:03:28.793
Wait for it, but only until the hedge time.

60
00:03:28.793 --> 00:03:33.905
If nothing came back, start the second call, and select whichever answers first.

61
00:03:33.905 --> 00:03:36.972
Then cancel the children, which stops the loser.

62
00:03:36.972 --> 00:03:43.873
Java with Reactor says it in one line: the first with a value, of the call, and the same call after a delay.

63
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:50.306
Forget that cancel line, and coroutineScope waits for the slow call anyway, which undoes the whole hedge.

64
00:03:50.306 --> 00:03:56.807
It's like getting your answer from the second branch, and then standing by the phone for the first one anyway.

65
00:03:58.033 --> 00:04:02.026
Now the guardrails, because a hedge is a duplicate request.

66
00:04:02.026 --> 00:04:02.296
One.

67
00:04:02.296 --> 00:04:05.003
Hedge only a call that's safe to repeat.

68
00:04:05.003 --> 00:04:07.980
Northline and Eastgate only look up a price.

69
00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.717
Fjord's price is a booking: each call holds a seat on one of its vans.

70
00:04:12.717 --> 00:04:16.100
Hedge Fjord, and you book two vans for one parcel.

71
00:04:16.333 --> 00:04:16.615
Two.

72
00:04:16.615 --> 00:04:23.434
Hedge only the courier whose tail hurts, here Northline, at a time taken from its measured speed.

73
00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:26.317
Too early, and every request gets hedged.

74
00:04:26.317 --> 00:04:28.285
That's the 400 calls we saw.

75
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:28.884
Three.

76
00:04:28.884 --> 00:04:34.390
The hedge takes its own permit, a token from Northline's limit of ten calls at a time.

77
00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:36.631
And here the cook's picture breaks.

78
00:04:36.631 --> 00:04:42.585
The other branch of a shop has its own staff, but our second call goes to the same Northline.

79
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.289
When Northline is swamped, a hedge only joins the queue that made it slow.

80
00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:52.264
So under full load, the hedge waits for a permit, and the first call usually wins.

81
00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:55.410
So, is a second question cheating?

82
00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.851
Not if it goes only to the slow tail.

83
00:04:57.851 --> 00:05:03.525
Wait until the usual time, ask once more, take the first answer, and cancel the other.

84
00:05:03.525 --> 00:05:05.571
A hedge is a duplicate request.

85
00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:09.925
Spend it on the slow tail, and only where a duplicate is harmless.

86
00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:10.984
Quick check.

87
00:05:10.984 --> 00:05:13.606
You hedge Fjord instead of Northline.

88
00:05:13.606 --> 00:05:14.740
What goes wrong?

89
00:05:17.933 --> 00:05:20.699
Every hedge holds a second seat on a van.

90
00:05:20.699 --> 00:05:25.691
Fjord's call is a booking, not safe to repeat, so it's asked exactly once.
